One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. This occurs when an application accepts untrusted data from a user and uses the `ObjectInputStream` to reconstruct an object. If a malicious class is present in the application's classpath, an attacker can craft a serialized object that executes arbitrary code during the deserialization process.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class DeserializationServer {
    public void handleRequest(Socket socket) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());

        // VULNERABLE: Deserializing data directly from an untrusted network source.
        // An attacker can send a crafted gadget chain to execute remote code (RCE).
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 

        System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.Socket;
import org.apache.commons.io.serialization.ValidatingObjectInputStream;

public class SecureDeserializationServer {
    public void handleRequest(Socket socket) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());

        // FIXED: Using a whitelist approach to validate classes before deserialization.
        // ValidatingObjectInputStream ensures only expected, safe classes are instantiated.
        try (ValidatingObjectInputStream vois = new ValidatingObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {
            // Only allow specific, known safe classes to be deserialized
            vois.accept(String.class, Integer.class, java.util.ArrayList.class);

            Object obj = vois.readObject();
            System.out.println("Received safe object: " + obj.toString());
        }
    }
}
```

